<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeju Island</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jejudo, Quelpart</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeju Island is a volcanic island off the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, the country's largest island, dominated by Mount Hallasan. It was the seat of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timna-yemen">Tamna</a> before its absorption by Goryeo, and in 1273 the last Sambyeolcho rebels were crushed there. In the Jeju uprising and its suppression in 1948–1949 tens of thousands of islanders were killed. It is now a self-governing province and a major resort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeju-island.jpg" alt="Jeju Island" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Simmon, using Landsat data provided by the United States Geological Survey. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeju_Island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court returned to Kaegyong in 1270, provoking the revolt of the Sambyeolcho units, crushed on <b>Jeju</b> in 1273.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Mumun culture that Korean archaeologists call Songguk-ri Culture is found in the Hoseo and Honam regions, western Yeongnam, the Ulsan and Gimhae areas and, since a find in 2005, deep in the interior of Gangwon Province; its farthest reach appears to have been <b>Jeju Island</b> and western Japan.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mumun-culture">Mumun culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Survivors under Kim Tong-jeong held out on <b>Jeju</b> until 1273.</blockquote><a href="/state/sambyeolcho">Sambyeolcho</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jeju</b> legend tells of three founders, ancestors of the Go, Yang and Bu clans, who emerged from holes in the ground at Samseonghyeol and married princesses who arrived by sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/tamna">Tamna</a></li><li><blockquote>The United States took the Korean question to the United Nations, which supervised elections in the south in May 1948, amid the <b>Jeju</b> uprising.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-army-military-government-in-korea">United States Army Military Government in Korea</a></li></ul></section>
